window.__SEARCH_INDEX__=[{"path": "about/", "title": "Our Commitment | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Our Commitment A campus where everybody is somebody Grambling State University is committed to fostering a campus culture defined by leadership, mutual respect, and student success. Eliminating hazing is essential to that promise. What We Stand For Our commitment to ending hazing Eliminating hazing is essential to strengthening our culture and ensuring the safety and dignity of every member of our community. Hazing is a crime under Louisiana law and a direct violation of University policy. Grambling State University strictly prohibits hazing in any form, against any individual, organization, or member of the campus community. This site provides clear guidance on what GSU stands for: belonging, accountability, and care for one another, as well as what behaviors we do not tolerate. Here, you will find education, prevention tools, reporting pathways, and support resources designed to keep our community informed and empowered. If you have a concern or need to report an incident, please visit the Report It page. Our Values The principles that guide us Belonging Every student deserves a place to grow and lead. Real community is built on respect, never on harm. Accountability We hold organizations and individuals to clear standards, and we report confirmed violations transparently. Care for one another We look out for each other. Support, recovery, and education are central to how we keep students safe. The People Behind Prevention The Hazing Prevention Team A multidisciplinary team of educators, staff, and faculty leads our prevention work year-round, providing education, support, expectations, and accountability across campus. Meet the Team Our Promise To be a place where everybody is somebody. Thank you for helping Grambling State University uphold our enduring promise. Report a concern, learn the signs, and stand with us. Report a Hazing Incident Learn About Hazing"}, {"path": "hazing-prevention-team/", "title": "Hazing Prevention Team | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Our Commitment Hazing Prevention Team Bringing campus partners together to address hazing, educate the community, and empower everyone to take action. Our Approach How the team works to shift campus culture The Hazing Prevention Team seeks to shift campus culture through prevention, early intervention, and continuous education. Prevention We stop hazing before it happens through ongoing programs that promote University rules, help students make informed decisions, provide alternative ways to grow community, and empower students to hold peers accountable. Identification & Action Being able to identify and act on hazing is key to early intervention. The team equips the community to recognize what hazing looks like, with clearly defined next steps and reporting resources. Continuous Education When an individual or organization is accused of hazing, the team provides necessary interventions and need-specific, research-based educational programs. Request a Prevention Presentation Membership Who serves on the team Membership is comprised of campus partners who support student organizations, who have direct involvement with the hazing investigation process, or who are tasked with supporting holistic student well-being. The Hazing Prevention Team is chaired by Tundra Turner and Charlotte Favors . If you have any questions, please reach out to them. Together, we can end hazing. Report a concern, request a presentation, or learn the signs. Every member of the community has a role. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}, {"path": "hazing-transparency-report/", "title": "Hazing Transparency Report | Grambling State University", "text": "Accountability & Transparency Hazing Transparency Report In accordance with the Stop Campus Hazing Act (SCHA), Grambling State University will maintain a Campus Hazing Transparency Report (CHTR). This report provides information on confirmed hazing violations by organizations formally recognized by the University. The CHTR will be updated biannually, as required. Each situation is unique, and all allegations are addressed on a case-by-case basis. The University's response to noted situations does not create binding precedents but may guide its handling of any similar situations that may arise in the future. How to navigate Navigate by year, then select the button with the name of the social organization whose violations you wish to view. Confirmed Violations Reports by year 2026 Kappa Alpha Psi View the confirmed violation report → Terminology How to read each report Each report uses the following fields to describe a confirmed violation. Incident Date of hazing incident or citation. Reported Date of report to the institution. NHQ Organization Inter/National Headquarters. Description General description of the hazing incident. Rules Violations University rules violations. Individuals Responsible Individuals involved or found responsible. Findings University findings. Arrests Arrests and arresting agency (not tracked with violations prior to 2024). Sanctions University sanctions. Rules Defined Standards from the Code of Student Conduct Please refer to the Revised Code of Student Conduct handbook (PDF) for the official standards by which students must conduct themselves. The handbook is published by the Office of Student Conduct; see also Student Conduct (Judicial Affairs) on gram.edu. Examples of conduct expectations addressed in the Code include: Hazing All forms of hazing are prohibited. For definitions, reporting, and related expectations on this site, see the GSU Hazing Policy summary . Threats and harm Conduct that threatens or harms people or property. Integrity Dishonesty or furnishing false information to University officials. Compliance Failure to comply with sanctions or directives imposed under the student conduct process. Published policies Violation of other University policies incorporated by reference in the Code. Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act, Annual Security & Fire Safety Report In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act, our institution publishes an Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (ASFR) each year by October 1. Beginning with the October 1, 2026 report, the ASFR will include data related to reported hazing incidents. This data, as with other Clery-reportable incidents, will encompass incidents reported to Campus Security Authorities (CSAs) or local police agencies that occurred within the institution's Clery geography. Know of something that isn't here? Report it. This report only reflects confirmed cases. If you suspect something, report it anonymously through the G Safe app. Report a Concern"}, {"path": "", "title": "Hazing Awareness & Prevention | Grambling State University", "text": "End Hazing at Grambling State Building a campus culture of respect and leadership At Grambling State University, ending hazing is part of our commitment to leadership development, student success, and community responsibility. We promote a campus where every student belongs, grows, and leads with integrity. Report an Incident What Is Hazing? Start Here Explore Hazing Awareness & Prevention at GSU Move through the site by what you need: understanding hazing, prevention, reporting, and support. Understanding Hazing Definitions, statistics, policies, and FAQs. Explore Prevention & Education Prevention steps, training, and guidance for families. Explore Reporting Concerns Anonymous and direct reporting options. Explore Student Support Resources Campus offices ready to help. Explore Transparency Report Confirmed hazing violations and institutional accountability. Explore Our Commitment & Community GSU values and the Hazing Prevention Team. Explore Anonymous Reporting See something? Report it. Submit anonymous safety concerns through the G Safe mobile app, or call 318-274-2507 (Dean of Students) or 318-274-2222 (GSUPD). Every report helps keep students safe. G Safe Mobile App Anonymous reporting, right from your phone. Get the app Dean of Students Talk through a concern with the Dean of Students office. 318-274-2507 Grambling State Police (GSUPD) For urgent or after-hours safety concerns. 318-274-2222 Scan to get G Safe Point your camera at the code to learn how to download the G Safe app for iOS or Android. About G Safe The Reality of Hazing Knowing the numbers is the first step to change 55% of students in clubs and organizations have been hazed. 91% of students who were hazed did not realize it was hazing. 95% of students who were hazed did not report it to campus officials. 74% of student-athletes experience some form of hazing. Sources: Allan & Madden, Hazing in View: College Students at Risk (2008); Hoover & Pollard (2000). View all hazing statistics Support for Everyone Wherever you fit in, there's a way to help Parents & Family Information for parents and families Consistent communication with your student is key to helping them avoid a hazing incident. Find advice to support your student. Find advice for your student Campus Support Support for hazing victims on campus Student Affairs connects students with whole-person support and recovery resources when they need help. Find help on campus Stand With Grambling Let's end hazing for good. Report a concern. Learn the signs. Lead the change. Together, we protect the legacy of every Grambling student. Report a Hazing Incident Learn About Hazing"}, {"path": "parents-and-family/", "title": "Parents & Family | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Parents & Family You're part of the prevention team It's important to speak with your student about hazing, because it can occur within any student group or organization. Consistent communication is one of the most powerful tools to prevent it. Start the Talk Start the conversation Speak respectfully Your student respects your judgment. A non-judgmental tone keeps the door open. Be persistent Move the conversation forward. Check in regularly, not just once. Understand the pull Belonging matters. Acknowledge the desire to fit in, and be there before harm happens. Report right away If you suspect hazing, make a report. You don't need proof to raise a concern. Questions to ask your student Are you in the process of joining an organization or team? How much time do you spend on new-member activities? Are you having trouble keeping up with academics? Are you being deprived of anything, such as sleep? What would you tell me about a hazing incident? Do you feel safe and respected in your organization? Trust Your Instincts Warning signs to watch for No single sign means hazing is happening, but several together are worth a closer conversation. Sudden secrecy Your student stops sharing details about meetings or activities. Unexplained injuries Bruises, exhaustion, or changes in how they look or move. Becoming isolated Pulling away from family, friends, or their usual support. Mood or sleep changes New anxiety, irritability, or disrupted sleeping and eating habits. Drop in grades Activities consuming all their time and hurting coursework. Odd hours & errands Always being asked to run errands or perform tasks at all hours. You're Not Alone How GSU supports your student Confidential care, advising, and recovery resources A team of Student Affairs staff ready to help Anonymous reporting through the G Safe app Transparent reporting that holds organizations accountable Contact Student Affairs Worried about your student? Reach out. You can make a report anonymously, or talk it through with the Dean of Students office. Report a Concern"}, {"path": "prevent-it/how-to-prevent/", "title": "How to Prevent | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Prevention & Education How to Prevent The fight against hazing is everyone's responsibility. These tips help students and community members know how to recognize, respond to, and proactively prevent hazing. Step One Speak Up The first step to ending hazing is to identify it and make your voice heard. Ask specific, clarifying questions if you see something that could be considered hazing. Ask questions, be direct “ I noticed you missed last night's meeting. You look tired and in pain, is everything ok? “ I saw you wearing a funny outfit in class today. Did someone force you to wear that? “ You haven't turned in your homework and your grades dropped. What's going on? “ Why do you have so many bruises on you? Did someone hit you? “ How did that symbol appear on your skin? Were you branded? “ We haven't seen you in a few weeks. Are you being forced to self-isolate? Remember, these conversations are always situational and contextual. You're more likely to get an honest response if the person trusts you. Even if they insist they aren't being hazed, hazing occurs with and without the victim's consent. Step Two Hazing Prevention Training Complete the free online Vector Solutions LMS training for hazing prevention. The full course is approximately 27 minutes long. ~27 min · Vector Solutions LMS Start the Training Step Three Alternate Activities There are many ways for members of a social organization to bond without hazing. Below are real options in the Lincoln Parish area that let members grow together. Outdoors & nature Lincoln Parish Park: trails, swimming, biking, picnics Jimmie Davis State Park: camping, hiking, lake activities Ruston downtown murals walk: public art in the city center Group nature photo challenge or outdoor yoga at a public park Arts & culture Eddie G. Robinson Museum: sports heritage Autrey House Museum: historic site near Dubach GSU campus art shows, lectures, and student programs Lincoln Parish Library: workshops and community programs Service & community Ruston Farmers Market: volunteer or shop together Community clean-ups or park beautification Service projects with churches, food pantries, or nonprofits Food drives and charity events with campus groups Food & social Downtown Ruston dining: small-group restaurant crawl Coffee shop meetups in Ruston or Grambling Group cooking or baking night with market ingredients Local bakeries and dessert spots: a walking dessert tour Games & fitness Bowling, escape rooms, or campus game nights Group fitness: Zumba, spin, or kickboxing studios Intramural scrimmages through university recreation Scavenger hunt across GSU campus or downtown Ruston"}, {"path": "prevent-it/", "title": "Prevent It | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Take Action Prevent It It's our job as a community to stand against hazing and build a culture of respect at Grambling State University. Find out how you can help prevent hazing on campus. Report a Concern Get Training Three Ways to Act It takes all of us to end hazing Ending hazing is a community effort. Choose the path that fits you: speaking up, getting trained, or helping build better traditions. Speak Up Recognize the warning signs and say something. Reporting early, even anonymously, can stop a harmful tradition before anyone gets hurt. Speak up & report Hazing Prevention Training Bring awareness to your organization. Training helps student leaders and members spot, interrupt, and prevent hazing before it starts. Request training Alternate Activities Build belonging without harm. Find team and trust-building ideas that strengthen bonds the right way. Explore ideas Prevention in Practice Four steps anyone can take 01 Know the signs Learn what isolation, pressure, and secrecy can look like in a club or team, even when it is framed as \"tradition.\" 02 Set the standard Make a culture of respect explicit. Leaders set the tone and members hold each other accountable. 03 Step in early Speak up before things escalate. A quiet word or a question early can change the outcome. 04 Report it Use the G Safe app or call. Reporting protects students and keeps the whole community safe. The People Behind Prevention Meet the Hazing Prevention Team A multidisciplinary team of educators, staff, and faculty providing year-round education, support, expectations, and accountability across campus. Hazing-prevention education across campus Confidential guidance for students and members Transparent reporting and institutional accountability Meet the Team Why Prevention Matters Hazing is more common, and more hidden, than most realize 1.5M high school students experience hazing each year. 47% of college students were already hazed back in high school. 25% of those hazed were first hazed before the age of 13. 71% of students have witnessed alcohol-related hazing. Sources: Allan & Madden, Hazing in View: College Students at Risk (2008); Hoover & Pollard (2000). View all hazing statistics Join the Movement Take the pledge to end hazing at Grambling Add your voice with the students, staff, and families committed to building a campus culture of respect and leadership. Report an Incident Get Training"}, {"path": "report-it/campus-support-services/", "title": "Campus Support Services | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Reporting Concerns Campus Support Services Several offices at Grambling State University are ready to talk through hazing-related concerns and connect students with help. Need emergency services? If you are in an emergency and need immediate assistance, call 911 or contact GSUPD. 318-274-2222 Where to Turn Offices that can help Find each office's location and contact information below. Student Engagement & Leadership The Office of Student Engagement and Leadership (OSEL) is the primary campus unit for student involvement, organizations, programming, and leadership development. Location: Favrot Student Union, 218 Phone: 318-274-3334 Get directions Counseling Center If you or someone you know has been hazed and is struggling mentally, the Counseling Center can help. Individual appointments are available Monday through Friday, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Location: Foster-Johnson, Room 113 Phone: 318-274-3163 / 318-274-3277 Counseling Center Crisis intervention Civil Rights & Title IX The Office of Civil Rights and Title IX offers counseling and support to all students. If you or someone you know has become a victim of hazing, these resources can help. Title IX home page Interview information Office of Student Conduct Location: 403 Main Street Phone: 318-274-7782 Email: turnert@gram.edu Incident Report Form Student Code of Conduct Ready to report or talk to someone? You can make an anonymous report through the G Safe app, or reach any office above directly. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}, {"path": "report-it/", "title": "Report It | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Speak Up Safely Report a Hazing Incident All members of the campus community have a duty to promptly report good-faith concerns about potential hazing. If you need it, your report can stay anonymous, and you don't need to be certain to report it. Anonymous Confidential 24/7 Three Ways to Report Choose the way that works for you G Safe Mobile App The fastest, fully anonymous option. Submit a safety concern in seconds, right from your phone. Get G Safe Online Report Form Access Student Conduct's online hazing report form. Reports using this form are not anonymous. Open the form Call & Talk to Someone Speak with the Dean of Students office or GSUPD to report or talk through a concern. Dean of Students 318-274-2507 GSUPD 318-274-2222 Prefer a national, anonymous line? Call the National Anti-Hazing Hotline, which forwards tips to institutions. 1-888-NOT-HAZE What Happens Next After you report 01 We receive it Your report reaches the Dean of Students or campus officials right away. 02 We review Trained staff assess the concern and determine the right support and next steps. 03 We support Affected students are connected to whole-person support and recovery resources. 04 We stay accountable Confirmed violations appear in our public transparency reporting."}, {"path": "violations/", "title": "Hazing Transparency Report | Hazing Awareness & Prevention", "text": "Hazing Transparency Report In accordance with the Stop Campus Hazing Act (SCHA), Grambling State University will maintain a Campus Hazing Transparency Report (CHTR). This report provides information on confirmed hazing violations by organizations formally recognized by the University. The CHTR will be updated biannually, as required. Each situation is unique, and all allegations are addressed on a case-by-case basis. The University’s response to noted situations does not create binding precedents but may guide its handling of any similar situations that may arise in the future. Navigate by year, then select the button with the name of the social organization whose violations you wish to view. 2025 2024 2023 2022 2021 Terminology Incident Date of Hazing Incident or Citation Reported Date of Report to Institution NHQ Organization Inter/National Headquarters Description General Description of Hazing Incident Rules Violations University Rules Violations Individuals Responsible Individuals Involved/Found Responsible Findings University Findings Arrests Arrests and arresting agency (not tracked with violations prior to 2024) Sanctions University Sanctions Rules Defined Please refer to the Revised Code of Student Conduct handbook (PDF) for the official standards by which students must conduct themselves. The handbook is published by the Office of Student Conduct; see also Student Conduct (Judicial Affairs) on gram.edu . Examples of conduct expectations addressed in the Code include: Hazing – All forms of hazing are prohibited. For definitions, reporting, and related expectations on this site, see the GSU Hazing Policy summary. Threats and harm – Conduct that threatens or harms people or property. Integrity – Dishonesty or furnishing false information to University officials. Compliance – Failure to comply with sanctions or directives imposed under the student conduct process. Published policies – Violation of other University policies incorporated by reference in the Code. 2025 Theta Chi Sigma Tau Gamma Sigma Pi Delta Kappa Epsilon Delta Sigma Phi 2024 Delta Tau Delta Pi Kappa Phi Kappa Sigma Zeta Psi Pi Kappa Alpha 2023 Sigma Chi Sigma Nu Alpha Tau Omega Sigma Alpha Epsilon Alpha Kappa Lambda 2022 Alpha Kappa Lambda Pi Kappa Alpha Zeta Beta Tau Kappa Alpha Theta Tau 2021 Delta Chi Phi Sigma Kappa Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act, our institution publishes an Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (ASFR) each year by October 1. Beginning with the October 1, 2026 report, the ASFR will include data related to reported hazing incidents. This data, as with other Clery-reportable incidents, will encompass incidents reported to Campus Security Authorities (CSAs) or local police agencies that occurred within the institution’s Clery geography."}, {"path": "what-is-hazing/faq/", "title": "Hazing FAQ | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Understanding Hazing Frequently Asked Questions Clear answers to common questions about hazing, the law, and how to report a concern. What is hazing? Hazing is when a person intentionally, knowingly, or recklessly causes, coerces, or forces another individual to engage in or endure certain actions for the purpose of pre-initiation activities, pledging, initiating, holding office, admitting, or affiliating into or with an organization, or for the purpose of continuing, reinstating, or enhancing an individual's membership or status in an organization. For a fuller description, visit the What Is Hazing page. Is hazing illegal? Yes. Hazing is considered a crime. Can I get sued for hazing? Yes. In addition to criminal penalties and University sanctions, an individual who engages in hazing could be sued and forced to pay significant monetary damages (along with attorney's fees) as part of a civil suit. These suits have resulted in judgements totaling millions of dollars. Isn't hazing ok if no one gets hurt? No. Any form of hazing, regardless of the outcome, is a crime in Louisiana and a violation of University policy. It does not become ok, legal, or permissible if no harm is intended or occurs. Verbal and emotional abuse can be just as damaging as physical abuse. Have people died from hazing? Sadly, yes. There are deaths attributable to hazing at other schools every year. In each of those situations, the individuals who engaged in the hazing probably thought what they were doing was harmless and would not result in someone's death. My group has always hazed new members. Isn't it a tradition? No. \"Tradition\" does not justify committing a crime or violating University policy. Traditions are created by groups, and groups hold the power to change or eliminate them. It only takes one year to break a hazing tradition. Remember that the founding members of organizations were not hazed. Our new members voluntarily participate, so that can't be hazing, right? Wrong. Under Louisiana state law and GSU policy, the fact that a person consented to a hazing activity is not a defense. The peer pressure and desire to belong in a student organization can create a coercive environment where true consent cannot be given. In short, an individual cannot consent to being hazed. Doesn't hazing teach respect and make better members? No. Respect is earned, not demanded or taught. When respect is earned, there is also trust. Hazing only motivates people out of fear and anxiety. Doesn't hazing help build a bond among new members? No. Hazing isolates each member from the established group. It does not promote organizational unity. There are safer and more productive ways to build friendships and community. Do I have a duty to report hazing? Yes. All members of the campus community have an obligation to promptly report any good-faith concerns about hazing that has occurred or will occur. If I know about hazing but don't promptly report it, what happens to me? If you are a student, you may be subject to charges and sanctions under the Code of Student Conduct. Employees may be subject to discipline. How do I report concerns about hazing? If the situation is an emergency and you or someone else needs immediate assistance, call 911 or contact GSUPD at 318-274-2222 . For non-emergency situations, see our reporting options . Any report should include as much detail as possible (who, where, when, and what happened). Still have a concern? You don't have to be certain. If something feels wrong, report it and we will take it from there. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}, {"path": "what-is-hazing/hazing-statistics/", "title": "Hazing Statistics | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Understanding Hazing Hazing Statistics Hazing is more common than most people realize, and no level of hazing is allowed or acceptable. National Research National hazing statistics Hazing is not a challenge unique to any one campus. It is common in sports, clubs, and student organizations across the nation. 55% of students in clubs and organizations have been hazed. 91% of students who were hazed did not realize it was hazing. 95% of students who were hazed did not report it. 1 in 10 students who were hazed labeled it as hazing. 74% of student-athletes experience some form of hazing. Presence of Alcohol Alcohol and hazing Alcohol is often involved in hazing incidents. 71% of students have witnessed alcohol-related hazing. 47% of student-athletes report hazing that involved drinking games. 82% of hazing deaths are alcohol-related. Before College It often starts earlier than you think Many students experience hazing even before college begins. 1.5M high school students experience hazing each year. 47% of college students were already hazed back in high school. 25% of those hazed were first hazed before the age of 13. Sources Where this data comes from National data was primarily sourced from the national study Hazing in View: College Students at Risk , conducted by Elizabeth Allan, Ph.D. and Mary Madden, Ph.D. Hoover and Pollard, 2000 Allan, E.J. and Madden, M., 2008 WITH US Center for Bystander Intervention, 2020 Knowing the numbers is the first step. If you suspect hazing, a confidential report can keep a fellow student safe. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}, {"path": "what-is-hazing/", "title": "What Is Hazing? | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Know It When You See It What Is Hazing? Hazing takes many forms and can be tricky to identify. It often hides behind tradition, secrecy, and the idea of \"earning\" your place. Learn the signs so you'll recognize it. Hazing is any activity expected of someone joining or participating in a group that humiliates, degrades, abuses, or endangers them, regardless of their willingness to participate. GSU Code of Student Conduct & StopHazing.org Understanding Severity The Spectrum of Hazing, it's all hazing Hazing exists on a spectrum, from subtle to severe. Behaviors at the \"milder\" end are easy to excuse, but they are exactly what escalate over time. Every level is still hazing. Intimidation Lower Severity Lies and purposeful deception Assigning demerits or punishment Forced silence Social isolation of new members Use of demeaning names Expecting members to carry certain items at all times Harassment Moderate Severity Verbal abuse Threats or implied threats Asking members to wear humiliating clothing Forced servitude \"Skit nights\" with humiliating acts Sleep deprivation Requiring new members to learn songs or chants Violence High Severity Forced or coerced use of food, alcohol, or other drugs Beating, paddling, or any form of physical assault Branding Staged kidnappings Sexual assault Forced consumption of vile substances All of This Is Hazing Examples of hazing Drawn directly from the GSU Code of Student Conduct. These actions qualify as hazing regardless of a person's willingness to participate. Substances & alcohol Forced or coerced consumption of food, alcohol, drugs, or any other substance Placement of undesirable substances on or in the body Deprivation of food or water Physical strain Unnecessary physical contact such as whipping, beating, paddling, or branding Calisthenics such as sit-ups, push-ups, or wall sits Unnecessary fatigue, physical activity, or sleep deprivation Control & servitude Servitude for personal benefit such as driving, cooking, cleaning, or running errands Required periods of silence or forced wearing of inappropriate apparel Lineups or events where individuals are yelled or cursed at Policy & Law The rules that prohibit hazing Hazing is a crime under Louisiana law and a direct violation of University policy. Read the standards that apply at Grambling State. GSU Hazing Policy Grambling State strictly prohibits all forms of hazing. Read the policy, definitions, and expectations. Read the policy GSU Code of Student Conduct The standards by which students must conduct themselves, published by the Office of Student Conduct. View the Code State Law Louisiana law defines hazing and makes it a criminal act. Learn what the law requires. Read the law Hazing Statistics National research on how common hazing is, and how often it goes unrecognized and unreported. See the data Frequently Asked Questions Clear answers to common questions about hazing, reporting, and what happens next. Read the FAQ Why It Matters Hazing is widespread, and easy to miss 91% of students who were hazed did not realize it was hazing. 1 in 10 students who were hazed correctly labeled it as hazing. 71% of students have witnessed alcohol-related hazing. 82% of hazing deaths are alcohol-related. Sources: Allan & Madden, Hazing in View: College Students at Risk (2008); Hoover & Pollard (2000). View all hazing statistics Think you're seeing hazing? Report it. You don't have to be certain. If something feels wrong, a confidential report can keep a fellow student safe. Report a Concern"}, {"path": "what-is-hazing/state-law/", "title": "State Law | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Understanding Hazing State Law Louisiana has a statewide, zero-tolerance approach to hazing, with anti-hazing laws and uniform policy guidance for Louisiana institutions. Louisiana Anti-Hazing Law How the law defines hazing Hazing is defined as follows: Any willful action taken or situation created, whether on or off any school, college, university, or other educational premises, which recklessly or intentionally endangers the mental or physical health of any student; or any willful act on or off any such premises by any person alone or acting with others in striking, beating, bruising, or maiming; or seriously offering, threatening, or attempting to strike, beat, bruise, or maim; or to do or seriously offer, threaten, or attempt to do physical violence to any student of any such educational institution; or any assault upon any such student made for the purpose of committing any of the acts, or producing any of the results, defined in this section. The term hazing does not include customary athletic events or similar contests or competitions, and is limited to those actions taken and situations created in connection with initiation into or affiliation with any organization. The term hazing does not include corporal punishment administered by officials or employees of public schools when in accordance with policies adopted by local boards of education. No person shall engage in what is commonly known and recognized as hazing, or encourage, aid, or assist any other person thus offending. No person shall knowingly permit, encourage, aid, or assist any person in committing the offense of hazing, or willfully acquiesce in the commission of such offense, or fail to report promptly his or her knowledge or any reasonable information within his or her knowledge of the presence and practice of hazing in this state to the chief executive officer of the appropriate educational institution. Any act of omission or commission shall be deemed hazing under the provisions of this section. Legislation Anti-hazing legislation The Louisiana Board of Regents maintains a current overview of Louisiana's anti-hazing legislation, including: Act 382 Act 635 Act 637 Act 640 Penalty Louisiana law defines hazing as a Class C misdemeanor. Individuals, organizations, and institutions found guilty of hazing, or of knowingly permitting hazing, forfeit their entitlement to public funds, scholarships, or awards, and will lose their approvals granted by the University. Credits: Acts 1981, No. 81-824, p. 1466, §§ 1-6. Louisiana Code 1975 § 16-1-23. Hazing is a crime. Report it. Reports can be made anonymously through the G Safe app or by calling GSUPD. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}, {"path": "what-is-hazing/ua-code-of-student-conduct/", "title": "GSU Code of Student Conduct | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Understanding Hazing GSU Code of Student Conduct The standards by which students are expected to conduct themselves at Grambling State University. The information below reflects the Office of Student Conduct as described on Grambling State University's Student Conduct (Judicial Affairs) page. The official rules are published in the Revised Code of Student Conduct handbook (PDF) . Purpose Purpose of the Office of Student Conduct The purpose of the Office of Student Conduct is to ensure that students will not be deprived of life, liberty, or property; to administer a fair student disciplinary process that adheres to prescribed standards; and to establish guidelines and rules to ensure that students maintain appropriate behavior at the University and off-campus. The Code of Student Conduct creates an expectation of behavior that the Institution deems acceptable. It is each student's responsibility to adhere to the policies and standards of conduct prescribed by the University, the Board of Supervisors for the University of Louisiana System, and applicable law. Goals What the Code aims to do Responsibility Promote student development by challenging and supporting students in owning individual responsibility. Growth Use educational strategies and disciplinary counseling as catalysts for self-understanding and personal growth. Civility Enforce a system characterized by accountability, compassion, and increased civility. Safe climate Promote a safe, orderly, and positive University climate through programming, training, and intervention. Resources Student conduct resources Incident Report Form Open the form Revised Code of Student Conduct View the handbook (PDF) Student Conduct (Judicial Affairs) Visit the office Have a concern to report? Use the online form, the G Safe app, or call the Dean of Students office. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}, {"path": "what-is-hazing/ua-hazing-policy/", "title": "GSU Hazing Policy | Grambling State University End Hazing", "text": "Understanding Hazing GSU Hazing Policy Grambling State University is committed to providing an environment that is free from all forms of hazing. Grambling State University is committed to providing an environment for students, employees, and campus visitors that is free from all forms of hazing. Hazing violates both policy and law and may lead to personal liability for the results of such behavior. What the Policy Covers The GSU Hazing Policy addresses these areas Prohibition on Hazing All forms of hazing are strictly prohibited, against any individual or organization in the campus community. Reporting Hazing Every member of the community has a duty to promptly report good-faith concerns about hazing. Prohibition on Retaliation Retaliation against anyone who reports hazing in good faith is prohibited. Read the full Hazing Policy (PDF) See something? Report it. Reports can be made anonymously through the G Safe app or by calling the Dean of Students office. Report a Concern What Is Hazing?"}];